\title{Fan-Structure in Selmer Rank Distributions:\\ Parity Rigidity and Gaussian-Binomial Layers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The statistical behavior of Selmer ranks of families of twisted elliptic curves --- in $p$-cyclic towers and in families of quadratic twists --- is governed by two independent structural mechanisms. First, along any tower in which the $p$-Selmer rank changes by exactly $\pm 1$ at each stage, the \emph{parity} of the rank is rigidly determined by the starting rank and the number of steps; it cannot fluctuate. This parity rigidity is the combinatorial source of the observed disparity between even and odd Selmer ranks. Second, the raw counts of rank-$k$ subgroups of a finite $\FF_q$-vector space --- the Gaussian binomial coefficients $\binom{n}{k}_q$ --- arrange themselves into a self-dual, unimodal ``fan'' of layers that degenerates to Pascal's triangle in the classical limit $q = 1$. We give precise statements and complete proof sketches of four results: a parity invariant for $\pm 1$ rank walks and its even-loop corollary; the finite support and dual recurrence of the Gaussian binomial coefficients; the self-duality $\binom{n}{k}_q = \binom{n}{n-k}_q$; and the classical limit $\binom{n}{k}_1 = \binom{n}{k}$, together with the $q$-integer formula for the rank-one layer.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Let $E$ be an elliptic curve over a number field, and for a prime $p$ let $\mathrm{Sel}_p(E)$ denote its $p$-Selmer group, a finite-dimensional vector space over $\FF_p$. Its dimension, the \emph{$p$-Selmer rank}, is an upper bound for the Mordell--Weil rank and is the computable proxy through which one studies the arithmetic of $E$ statistically. Two settings produce large families of curves whose Selmer ranks one wishes to understand distributionally: $p$-cyclic towers of field extensions, in which the Selmer rank evolves stage by stage; and quadratic-twist families, in which adding a ramified prime perturbs the Selmer group.

In both settings, the theory of Klagsbrun, Mazur, and Rubin predicts a precise limiting distribution of Selmer ranks --- one exhibiting a marked \emph{disparity} between even and odd ranks. This paper isolates two mechanisms that, together, account for the shape of these distributions: a rigidity phenomenon governing how a single rank moves, and a combinatorial model for how the population of ranks is distributed across a finite vector space.

\section{Parity Rigidity of Rank Walks}

\begin{definition}[Rank walk]
A \emph{rank walk} is a sequence $w : \NN \to \ZZ$ such that for every index $i$,
\[
w(i+1) = w(i) + 1 \quad\text{or}\quad w(i+1) = w(i) - 1.
\]
\end{definition}

A rank walk models a sequence of $p$-Selmer ranks along a tower or twist family, encoding the fact that each elementary step changes the rank by a single unit. The definition is non-vacuous: e.g.\ $w(i) = i$ is a rank walk.

\begin{theorem}[Parity rigidity]\label{thm:parity}
Let $w$ be a rank walk. Then for every $n \in \NN$,
\[
w(n) \equiv w(0) + n \pmod 2.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$. For $n = 0$ the statement is $w(0) \equiv w(0)$. Assume $w(n) \equiv w(0) + n \pmod 2$. By definition either $w(n+1) = w(n)+1$ or $w(n+1) = w(n)-1$; in both cases $w(n+1) \equiv w(n) + 1 \pmod 2$ because $-1 \equiv +1 \pmod 2$. Hence $w(n+1) \equiv w(0) + (n+1) \pmod 2$.
\end{proof}

The direction of each step is irrelevant to the parity; only the number of steps matters.

\begin{corollary}[Even-loop obstruction]\label{cor:even}
Let $w$ be a rank walk. If $w(n) = w(0)$ for some $n \in \NN$, then $n$ is even.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}, $w(n) \equiv w(0)+n \pmod 2$. The hypothesis $w(n)=w(0)$ gives $n \equiv 0 \pmod 2$, i.e.\ $2 \mid n$.
\end{proof}

\emph{Interpretation.} A rank walk cannot return to its starting value in an odd number of steps. In arithmetic terms, one residue class of ranks is structurally excluded once the parity and step count are fixed; this is the rigidity underlying the even/odd disparity in Selmer ranks.

\section{The Selmer Fan: Gaussian-Binomial Layers}

Since the $p$-Selmer group is a finite $\FF_p$-vector space, the relevant combinatorial datum is the number of rank-$k$ subspaces of $\FF_q^n$, the Gaussian binomial coefficient.

\begin{definition}[Gaussian binomial coefficient]
For a natural number $q$ define $\binom{n}{k}_q$ by the \emph{forward $q$-Pascal recurrence}:
\[
\tbinom{0}{0}_q = 1,\quad \tbinom{0}{k+1}_q = 0,\quad \tbinom{n+1}{0}_q = 1,
\]
\[
\tbinom{n+1}{k+1}_q = \tbinom{n}{k}_q + q^{\,k+1}\tbinom{n}{k+1}_q.
\]
\end{definition}

When $q$ is a prime power, $\binom{n}{k}_q$ equals the number of $k$-dimensional $\FF_q$-subspaces of $\FF_q^n$. For fixed $n$, the sequence $\bigl(\binom{n}{k}_q\bigr)_{k=0}^{n}$ is the \emph{Selmer fan}.

\begin{lemma}[Boundary values]
For all $q,n$: $\binom{n}{0}_q = 1$; for all $q,k$: $\binom{0}{k+1}_q = 0$; and the recurrence $\binom{n+1}{k+1}_q = \binom{n}{k}_q + q^{\,k+1}\binom{n}{k+1}_q$ holds definitionally.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Finite support]\label{thm:support}
If $n < k$ then $\binom{n}{k}_q = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$. For $n=0$, $k \ge 1$, the value is $0$ by definition. For the step, if $k > n+1$ write $k = k'+2$ with $k'+1 > n$; the recurrence expresses $\binom{n+1}{k}_q$ as a combination of $\binom{n}{k'+1}_q$ and $\binom{n}{k'+2}_q$, both zero by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Finite support reflects that $\FF_q^n$ has no subspace of dimension exceeding $n$; it is a prerequisite for the dual recurrence, since natural-number subtraction truncates at zero.

\begin{theorem}[Dual $q$-Pascal recurrence]\label{thm:dual}
For all $q,n,k$,
\[
\tbinom{n+1}{k+1}_q = q^{\,n-k}\tbinom{n}{k}_q + \tbinom{n}{k+1}_q.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $n < k$ both sides reduce to $\binom{n}{k+1}_q$ via Theorem~\ref{thm:support}. Otherwise $k \le n$; induct on $n$. Apply the forward recurrence, the inductive hypothesis on $\binom{n}{k}_q$ and $\binom{n}{k+1}_q$, and reconcile powers of $q$ via $q^{\,n-k}\cdot q = q^{\,n+1-k}$, with careful truncated-subtraction bookkeeping.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-duality]\label{thm:symm}
For every $q$ and all $k \le n$,
\[
\tbinom{n}{k}_q = \tbinom{n}{n-k}_q.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $n$. The cases $n=0$, $k=0$, $k=n$ follow from boundary values and finite support. For $0 < k < n$, expand the left side by the forward recurrence and the right side by the dual recurrence (Theorem~\ref{thm:dual}):
\[
\tbinom{n}{k}_q = \tbinom{n-1}{k-1}_q + q^{k}\tbinom{n-1}{k}_q,\qquad
\tbinom{n}{n-k}_q = q^{(n-1)-(n-k)}\tbinom{n-1}{n-k-1}_q + \tbinom{n-1}{n-k}_q.
\]
The inductive hypothesis identifies $\binom{n-1}{k-1}_q = \binom{n-1}{n-k}_q$ and $\binom{n-1}{k}_q = \binom{n-1}{n-k-1}_q$, while $(n-1)-(n-k) = k-1$ matches the exponents; the two expressions coincide.
\end{proof}

Self-duality expresses the bijection between a $k$-dimensional subspace and its $(n-k)$-dimensional orthogonal complement, pinning the axis of symmetry at $k = n/2$.

\begin{theorem}[Classical limit]\label{thm:one}
At $q = 1$, $\binom{n}{k}_1 = \binom{n}{k}$, the ordinary binomial coefficient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At $q=1$ the forward recurrence becomes Pascal's rule $\binom{n+1}{k+1}_1 = \binom{n}{k}_1 + \binom{n}{k+1}_1$ with the same boundary values; by induction $\binom{n}{k}_1$ satisfies the defining recurrence of $\binom{n}{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Rank-one layer is the $q$-integer]
For all $n \ge 1$,
\[
\tbinom{n}{1}_q = 1 + q + q^2 + \dots + q^{\,n-1} = \frac{q^n-1}{q-1}\quad(q \ne 1).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The forward recurrence with $k=0$ gives $\binom{n+1}{1}_q = 1 + q\binom{n}{1}_q$, with $\binom{1}{1}_q = 1$; the linear recurrence solves to $[n]_q$.
\end{proof}

\section{Numerical Illustrations}

For $q=2$, $n=4$: $\binom{4}{\cdot}_2 = 1,15,35,15,1$ (self-dual). For $q=3$, $n=3$: $1,13,13,1$. At $q=1$: $\binom{4}{\cdot}_1 = 1,4,6,4,1$. The rank-one layer $\binom{4}{1}_3 = 1+3+9+27 = 40$. The four length-$2$ rank walks from $0$ --- $0\to1\to0$, $0\to1\to2$, $0\to-1\to0$, $0\to-1\to-2$ --- all end at values $\equiv 0 \pmod 2$.

\section{Discussion and Future Directions}

Parity rigidity constrains the \emph{support} of a rank distribution to a single parity lattice; the Gaussian fan describes the \emph{shape} of the mass on the surviving lattice. Their conjunction turns qualitative heuristics into exact statements. Future work includes: strict unimodality of the fan with a sharp central plateau; a two-point limiting law under parity conditioning; monotone $q$-interpolation of the normalized layer weights between the binomial heuristic ($q=1$) and the arithmetic count ($q=p$); and an explicit disparity constant expressed through the boundary layers of the fan.

